WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.480
Brush a forearm slowly, then at a caress, then fast.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.920
One touch fibre seems to care how fast you stroke.

3
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:12.340
Could that be why a caress feels nice?

4
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:14.780
Put the speeds in your hand.

5
00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:17.520
Mark a 10 cm path on the forearm.

6
00:00:18.550 --> 00:00:23.890
At 3 cm/s, a brush crosses it in about 3 seconds: a slow caress.

7
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.980
Ten times faster is a quick swipe.

8
00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.380
Ten times slower is a crawl of over half a minute.

9
00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:35.280
A low-threshold mechanoreceptor responds to gentle force.

10
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:40.480
Hairy skin has fast, myelinated ones and slow, unmyelinated ones.

11
00:00:41.483 --> 00:00:46.103
A C-tactile afferent is a human one with a thin, unmyelinated axon.

12
00:00:46.843 --> 00:00:47.843
It conducts slowly.

13
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:53.993
Microneurography records single axons from a skin nerve in an awake person.

14
00:00:55.017 --> 00:01:00.357
In 2009, Löken and colleagues recorded this way while a soft brush crossed the

15
00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:01.397
forearm at set speeds.

16
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:07.140
The recorded myelinated touch fibres fired harder the faster the brush moved.

17
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:13.650
Predict. If C-tactile fibres worked the same way, what shape would their curve have?

18
00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:15.790
What shape would show a preference?

19
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:27.230
Their firing traced an inverted U. It was highest for strokes between about 1 and 10

20
00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:28.230
cm/s.

21
00:01:29.233 --> 00:01:31.513
Slower and faster strokes drove them less.

22
00:01:35.033 --> 00:01:38.053
A separate group of volunteers rated the same strokes.

23
00:01:38.973 --> 00:01:42.553
Their average pleasantness had the same shape, highest over the same range.

24
00:01:43.583 --> 00:01:48.263
Of the fibre classes recorded, only the C-tactile afferent had a curve of that

25
00:01:48.263 --> 00:01:49.263
shape.

26
00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:50.803
Why does that matter?

27
00:01:51.823 --> 00:01:55.403
Suppose only fibres whose firing rises with speed drove the ratings.

28
00:01:56.517 --> 00:02:00.197
If more firing meant more pleasant, ratings would rise with speed.

29
00:02:01.197 --> 00:02:02.197
They do not.

30
00:02:02.817 --> 00:02:05.957
So the caress-tuned fibre is the obvious candidate.

31
00:02:06.617 --> 00:02:09.357
But a candidate is a suspect, not a proven cause.

32
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.180
Ratings and recordings came from different people.

33
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.460
And other signals combined could, in principle, make the same curve.

34
00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:23.850
The one idea: of the fibres recorded in human hairy skin, the C-tactile afferent

35
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.370
fired most at caress speeds.

36
00:02:26.483 --> 00:02:28.643
Average pleasantness follows its curve.

37
00:02:29.143 --> 00:02:32.803
So its input is the prime candidate for the pleasant side of touch.

38
00:02:33.867 --> 00:02:37.127
Only taking the input away can show whether it is needed.
